ames/71"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Cross-Country, Individual, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "15 July 1912 — 14:15"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/233"} "Stockholms Olympiastadion, Stockholm"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/56649"} "45 from 10 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "circa 12,000 metres. Two laps with a start and finish inthe Olympic Stadium. The runners ran ¼ lap in the Stadium, returned to the Stadium later and ran anotehr ½ lap, and eventually finished with ¾ lap inside the Stadium."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "This was the first time that a cross-country event was held on the Olympic programme, although it would also be conducted in " [:a {:href "/results/57133"} "1920"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/57406"} "1924"] ".  The course was not revealed to the participants prior to the start, but was marked just before the race began with red ribbons.  It was very hilly, through the forest with steep inclines and descents, and many natural obstacles.  The British and Continental runners were not used to this kind of terrain, and the race was dominated by the Swedes and Finns."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The international cross-country championship had been won 1911 by " [:a {:href "/athletes/68086"} "Jean Bouin"] ", who would also win in 1912-1913.  But " [:a {:href "/athletes/67641"} "Hannes Kolehmainen"] " had already won the " [:a {:href "/results/56634"} "5,000"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/56642"} "10,000 metres"] " at Stockholm and, when he elected to run the cross-country, he had to be favored.  Kolehmainen won easily by over 30 seconds.  Two Swedes, " [:a {:href "/athletes/76047"} "Hjalmar Andersson"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/76115"} "John Eke"] ", won the silver and bronze medals, respectively.  Bouin ran in this race, but did not finish."